  "summary_md": "The furnishings batch is framed by a weak housing and consumer backdrop, but the real story is starkly divergent strategic moves. AMWD’s pending MasterBrand acquisition introduces M&A uncertainty atop an earnings rebound. SNBR is scrambling for survival, using covenant relief and a messy product-line reset to buy time. At the far end of the spectrum, ATER and XWIN are exiting their core businesses entirely, pivoting into critical minerals and AI/SpaceX investments in pivots that are as promotional as they are transformational.\n\nOther common threads: tariff-related benefits masking demand softness (COOK, HBB), restructuring through distribution consolidation (LCUT) and portfolio simplification (HOFT), and share buybacks where management sees value despite sluggish sales (LOVE, FLXS). The batch highlights a sector where strategic repositioning—via M&A, radical reinvention, or cost-cutting—is dominating the narrative amid an uneven macro recovery.",
